course: dictionary
lesson: 806
title: Begin and its family of words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To begin means to start. You can begin a race, begin a story, or begin your homework. «The film begins at 7» means the film starts at 7 o'clock. Use the words around begin to check: a start time, a first step, or a new activity all tell you it means start.
explain: Begin can also mean something starts to exist or happen. «Once upon a time» is a phrase you often hear when a story begins. When you see «to begin with», think first: «To begin with, we need paper» means the first thing we need is paper.
explain: Begin changes its form to show time. Today you begin, yesterday you began, and you have begun before. The -ing form is beginning, as in «the beginning of the book». A beginner is someone who is just starting to learn something.
fact: To begin means to start doing something, or to start happening.
fact: «To begin with» means first, or at the start.
fact: The forms of begin are begins, began, begun and beginning.
fact: Beginning doubles its n because the stress falls on the last part: be-GIN, beginning.
fact: A beginner is someone who is just starting to learn something.
exercise: «The film begins at 7.» What does begins mean here? | *starts | ends | stops | forgets
exercise: «To begin with, we need paper.» What does to begin with mean? | never | *first, at the start | at the end | in the middle
exercise: What is the past tense of begin? | beginned | *began | begining | begin
test: «To find how many there were at the start, begin at the end and undo each step in the opposite order.» What does begin mean in this sentence? | finish at | sleep at | stop at | *start from
test: Which word is the -ing form of begin? | begins | *beginning | began | begun
exam: Which sentence uses begin correctly? | *The story begins once upon a time. | The story beginning begun. | The story began once upon a begin. | The story begin yesterday.
